# <%= current_page.data.title %>

The Integration Hub file transfer service receives a file, checks it for
malware and sends it to the right destination. This happens automatically after
you upload a file using SFTP or FTPS.

## What happens after you upload a file

1. Your file arrives in a private receiving area. The service confirms that it
   has received the exact version that you uploaded.
2. The file moves to a secure processing area for a malware scan.
3. The scan result determines where the file goes next:
   * clean files move to the receiving service's destination
   * files that may contain malware move to quarantine
   * files with an unclear or unsupported scan result move to an investigation
     area
4. For a clean file, the Integration Hub can start the configured action or
   send the configured notification to the receiving service.

At each stage, the service checks that the destination has the exact file that
was uploaded before removing the source copy. Your uploaded file is therefore
moved through the service; it does not remain in the upload folder after it has
moved to processing. The same applies when the file moves from processing to
its clean, quarantine or investigation destination.

## What you need to do

Wait for your file-transfer client to confirm the upload has completed before
closing it. Keep your local copy until the receiving service confirms it has
processed the file.

You cannot view, download or replace a file after upload. This protects the
integrity of the transfer and helps the receiving service work with the file it
expects.

## If the receiving service cannot find a file

First check that the upload completed and that you used the correct username,
environment and destination folder. Then contact the receiving service with the
file name, upload time and environment.

If the receiving service asks you to involve the Integration Hub team, post in
`#ask-integration-hub`. Include the username, file name, upload time with time
zone, environment and protocol used. Do not share passwords or private keys.
